{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Final Keyword in Java "}, {"type": "position", "content": 7}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when talking about Java programming."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Talk about principles, concepts, characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob07re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Be Supposed To: Affirmative Form"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>When we want to express that something is expected or required from someone or something, we can use the structure <strong>be supposed to</strong>.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p><strong>Be supposed to</strong> in its present simple form is used to say that something is expected or required to happen.</p> <p>Take a look at the following structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>Subject + am/is/are + supposed to + verb base form + complement</strong></p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>Final in Java <strong>is supposed to help</strong> programmers create tighter and more readable code.</i></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'm supposed to have finished this new video game, but it isn't working as it's supposed to.  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "All these are supposed to ensure security and it's hard to hack. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Apart from that, codes are supposed to be readable for humans as well."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Would Rather for What Is Preferable in Negative Form"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>Use <strong>would rather not </strong>to express that it is not preferable to do something.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>Would rather not is usually used to say that you would not prefer to do something.</p> <p>Structure:</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject +<strong> would rather </strong>+ <strong>not </strong>+ base form of the verb</p> <p>Example:</p> <p><i>I<strong>&rsquo;d rather not use</strong> unreadable codes. </i>&nbsp;</p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "However, you'd rather not spread final keywords all over your code."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You'd rather not work alone. Remember that writing a code is like writing any other thing."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "override "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to ignore  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: When it is assigned to a method, it cannot be overridden by any subclasses."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob07aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "class "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a blueprint that describes the behavior that an object of its type has"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I thought that the main purpose of final keywords, when assigned to a class, is that it cannot be extended."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob07aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "subclass "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a class that has inherited the code from a previous class   "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It cannot be overridden by any subclasses, so its content cannot be changed by an outsider."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob07aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "constant "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a variable that has been assigned as final so it never changes   "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There are some good uses of final, such as the occasional final constant."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob07aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "readable "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "easy to read  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: A readable code is important because it is executable."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob07aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for information about examples of final keywords in java syntax. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write notes and create a poster which you explains each part of the syntax of the final. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share it with your partner and answer all his questions."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for information about examples of final keywords in java syntax."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a poster which you explains each part of the syntax of the final. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I talk about final keywords syntax in Java."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob07re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<p><strong>Assistant</strong>: Good morning, Mr. Jones. I&rsquo;m supposed to have finished this new video game, but it isn&rsquo;t working as it&rsquo;s supposed to.&nbsp;</p>\n<p><strong>Programmer</strong>: Let me have a look. Hmm, OK. First of all, you have written too many final keywords.</p>\n<p><strong>Assistant</strong>: I thought that the main purpose of final keywords, when assigned to a class, is that it cannot be extended and when assigned to a method, it cannot be overridden by any subclasses, so its content cannot be changed by an outsider. In the case of a variable, it cannot be reassigned to a different object. All these are supposed to ensure security and it&rsquo;s hard to hack.</p>"}, {"time": "45000", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob07re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<p><strong>Programmer</strong>: That&rsquo;s perfect. However, you&rsquo;d rather not spread final keywords all over your code. There are some good uses of final, such as the occasional final constant.</p>\n<p><strong>Assistant</strong>: So, shall I change it all?</p>\n<p><strong>Programmer</strong>: Not all. There is some good stuff here. Make it work and then clean it.</p>"}, {"time": "68000", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob07re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<p><strong>Assistant</strong>: Thank you, Sir. However, I know that a clean code is quite difficult to achieve.</p>\n<p><strong>Programmer</strong>: You&rsquo;d rather not work alone. Remember that writing a code is like writing any other thing. First, you make a draft and then you check it. Work with another person that can proofread your work. A readable code is important because it is executable. Apart from that, codes are supposed to be readable for humans as well. A clean code makes it easier for other people to enhance it. I know that codes are tighter than any other piece of writing but try to keep it simple; for example, in this case, a function is supposed to do just one thing.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le02